              </annotation>
              </annotations>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw5o-combine">
    <title>Three Integrals Into One</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Combine the three integrals of
        <xref ref="ex-hw5o-combine"/> into a single polar integral. In
        <m>\displaystyle\int\!\!\int r^3\sin\theta\cos\theta\,
        dr\,d\theta</m>, the radius runs from <var width="6"/> to
        <var width="6"/> and <m>\theta</m> from <var width="6"/> to
        <var width="8"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var>
        <condition number="1" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
         <condition string=".*">
     <feedback>
           <p>Incorrect.</p>
         </feedback>
   </condition>
 </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="2" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
              <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect.</p>
              </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="0" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
              <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect.</p>
              </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="0.785" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
              <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect.</p>
              </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw5-solutions">
      <p>
        The three pieces together are the part of the annulus
        <m>1 \le r \le 2</m> lying between the <m>x</m>-axis and the line
        <m>y = x</m>. Since <m>xy = r^2\sin\theta\cos\theta</m> and
        <m>dA = r\,dr\,d\theta</m> by
        <xref ref="eq-polar-area-element"/>,
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_0^{\pi/4}\!\!\int_1^2 r^3 \sin\theta\cos\theta
        \,dr\,d\theta .
      </me>
      <figure xml:id="fig-hw5o-combine-region">
        <caption>
          The three regions of integration in <xref ref="ex-hw5o-combine"/>, labelled I, II, and III from left to right, with the dashed lines <m>x = 1/\sqrt2</m>, <m>x = 1</m>, and <m>x = \sqrt2</m> that separate them. Together they make up the part of the annulus <m>1 \le r \le 2</m> between the <m>x</m>-axis and the line <m>y = x</m>.
        </caption>
        <image width="75%">
          <shortdescription>
          A shaded sector of an annulus between the circles of radius one and two, from the x axis up to the line y equals x, split into three vertical strips by dashed lines.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
            Coordinate axes with blue quarter circles of radius one and two and the blue line y equals x. The region between the two circles and between the x axis and the line is shaded tan. Dashed orange vertical lines at x equals one over root two, x equals one, and x equals root two divide it into three pieces labelled I, II, and III: piece I, between x equals one over root two and one, lies between the small circle and the line; piece II, between x equals one and root two, lies between the x axis and the line; piece III, between x equals root two and two, lies between the x axis and the large circle.
            </p>
          </description>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-hw5o-combine">
            <diagram dimensions="(380,288)" margins="10">
              <definition>c1(t) = (cos(t), sin(t))</definition>
              <definition>c2(t) = (2*cos(t), 2*sin(t))</definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-0.35,-0.35,2.55,1.85)">
              <grid-axes at="cb-grid" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" hlabels="(1,2,1)" vlabels="(1,1,1)"/>
              <polygon at="cb-region" fill="#fbe3d0" stroke="none" closed="yes" points="((2,0),(2,0.02618),(1.999,0.05235),(1.998,0.07852),(1.997,0.1047),(1.996,0.1308),(1.994,0.1569),(1.992,0.183),(1.989,0.2091),(1.986,0.2351),(1.983,0.2611),(1.979,0.287),(1.975,0.3129),(1.971,0.3387),(1.967,0.3645),(1.962,0.3902),(1.956,0.4158),(1.951,0.4414),(1.945,0.4669),(1.938,0.4923),(1.932,0.5176),(1.925,0.5429),(1.918,0.568),(1.91,0.5931),(1.902,0.618),(1.894,0.6429),(1.885,0.6676),(1.876,0.6922),(1.867,0.7167),(1.858,0.7411),(1.848,0.7654),(1.838,0.7895),(1.827,0.8135),(1.816,0.8373),(1.805,0.861),(1.794,0.8846),(1.782,0.908),(1.77,0.9312),(1.758,0.9543),(1.745,0.9772),(1.732,1),(1.719,1.023),(1.705,1.045),(1.691,1.067),(1.677,1.089),(1.663,1.111),(1.648,1.133),(1.633,1.154),(1.618,1.176),(1.603,1.197),(1.587,1.218),(1.571,1.238),(1.554,1.259),(1.538,1.279),(1.521,1.299),(1.504,1.319),(1.486,1.338),(1.469,1.358),(1.451,1.377),(1.433,1.396),(1.414,1.414),(0.7071,0.7071),(0.7163,0.6978),(0.7254,0.6884),(0.7343,0.6788),(0.7431,0.6691),(0.7518,0.6593),(0.7604,0.6494),(0.7688,0.6394),(0.7771,0.6293),(0.7853,0.6191),(0.7934,0.6088),(0.8013,0.5983),(0.809,0.5878),(0.8166,0.5771),(0.8241,0.5664),(0.8315,0.5556),(0.8387,0.5446),(0.8457,0.5336),(0.8526,0.5225),(0.8594,0.5113),(0.866,0.5),(0.8725,0.4886),(0.8788,0.4772),(0.885,0.4656),(0.891,0.454),(0.8969,0.4423),(0.9026,0.4305),(0.9081,0.4187),(0.9135,0.4067),(0.9188,0.3947),(0.9239,0.3827),(0.9288,0.3706),(0.9336,0.3584),(0.9382,0.3461),(0.9426,0.3338),(0.9469,0.3214),(0.9511,0.309),(0.955,0.2965),(0.9588,0.284),(0.9625,0.2714),(0.9659,0.2588),(0.9692,0.2462),(0.9724,0.2334),(0.9753,0.2207),(0.9781,0.2079),(0.9808,0.1951),(0.9833,0.1822),(0.9856,0.1693),(0.9877,0.1564),(0.9897,0.1435),(0.9914,0.1305),(0.9931,0.1175),(0.9945,0.1045),(0.9958,0.0915),(0.9969,0.07846),(0.9979,0.0654),(0.9986,0.05234),(0.9992,0.03926),(0.9997,0.02618),(0.9999,0.01309),(1,0))"/>
              <parametric-curve at="cb-inner" function="c1" domain="(0,pi/2)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
              <parametric-curve at="cb-outer" function="c2" domain="(0,pi/2)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
              <line at="cb-diag" endpoints="((0,0),(1.6,1.6))" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
              <line at="cb-split1" endpoints="((1,0),(1,1))" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2" dash="5 4"/>
              <line at="cb-split2" endpoints="((1.414,0),(1.414,1.414))" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2" dash="5 4"/>
              <line at="cb-split0" endpoints="((0.7071,0),(0.7071,0.7071))" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2" dash="5 4"/>
              <point at="cb-p0" p="(0.7071,0.7071)" fill="black" size="3"/>
              <point at="cb-p2" p="(1.414,1.414)" fill="black" size="3"/>
              <label at="cb-x0" anchor="(0.7071,0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-4)" color="#D55E00" clear-background="yes"><m>\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}</m></label>
              <label at="cb-x2" anchor="(1.414,0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-4)" color="#D55E00" clear-background="yes"><m>\sqrt2</m></label>
              <label at="cb-r1" anchor="(0.5,0.95)" alignment="ne" color="#0072B2"><m>r=1</m></label>
              <label at="cb-r2" anchor="(1.0,1.73)" alignment="ne" color="#0072B2"><m>r=2</m></label>
              <label at="cb-diaglab" anchor="(1.62,1.62)" alignment="ne" offset="(2,2)" color="#0072B2"><m>y=x</m></label>
              <label at="cb-I" anchor="(0.9,0.64)" alignment="c"><m>\mathrm{I}</m></label>
              <label at="cb-II" anchor="(1.2,0.5)" alignment="c"><m>\mathrm{II}</m></label>
              <label at="cb-III" anchor="(1.68,0.5)" alignment="c"><m>\mathrm{III}</m></label>
              <label at="cb-x3" anchor="(2,0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-4)"><m>2</m></label>
              </coordinates>
              <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure" text="The sector of the annulus between radii one and two and between the x axis and the line y equals x, split into the three regions of the given integrals.">
                <annotation ref="cb-region" text="The shaded region, the union of the three regions of integration."/>
                <annotation ref="cb-inner" text="The inner circle r equals one." sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="cb-outer" text="The outer circle r equals two." sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="cb-diag" text="The line y equals x, which is the ray theta equals pi over four."/>
                <annotation ref="cb-split1" text="The dashed line x equals one, between regions one and two."/>
                <annotation ref="cb-split2" text="The dashed line x equals root two, between regions two and three."/>
              </annotation>
              </annotations>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw5o-two-circles">
    <title>Mass Between Two Circles</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the mass of the region bounded by <m>x^2+y^2 = 2x</m> and
        <m>x^2+y^2 = 2y</m>, whose density is <m>\rho = 1/x</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Away from the origin the two circles cross at the polar angle
        <m>\theta = </m> <var width="8"/>, and the mass is
        <var width="10"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var>
        <condition number="0.785" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
         <condition string=".*">
     <feedback>
           <p>Incorrect.</p>
         </feedback>
   </condition>
 </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="2.264" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
              <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect.</p>
              </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw5-solutions">
      <p>
        The two circles are <m>r = 2\sin\theta</m> and
        <m>r = 2\cos\theta</m>, meeting where
        <m>\theta = \pi/4</m>. With
        <m>\rho = \frac{1}{r\cos\theta}</m> and
        <m>dA = r\,dr\,d\theta</m> by
        <xref ref="eq-polar-area-element"/>,
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          M \amp = \int_0^{\pi/4}\!\!\int_0^{2\sin\theta}
          \frac{r\,dr\,d\theta}{r\cos\theta}
          + \int_{\pi/4}^{\pi/2}\!\!\int_0^{2\cos\theta}
          \frac{r\,dr\,d\theta}{r\cos\theta}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = 2\int_0^{\pi/4}\tan\theta\,d\theta
          + 2\int_{\pi/4}^{\pi/2} d\theta
          = \ln 2 + \frac{\pi}{2} \approx 2.264 .
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <figure xml:id="fig-hw5o-two-circles-region">
        <caption>
          The region of <xref ref="ex-hw5o-two-circles"/>, inside both circles <m>r = 2\cos\theta</m> and <m>r = 2\sin\theta</m>. The dashed ray <m>\theta = \pi/4</m> through <m>(1,1)</m> splits it: below it a ray leaves the region on <m>r = 2\sin\theta</m>, above it on <m>r = 2\cos\theta</m>.
        </caption>
        <image width="60%">
          <shortdescription>
          Two unit circles, one centered at one comma zero and one at zero comma one, with their lens-shaped overlap shaded and the diagonal through the origin and one comma one drawn dashed.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
            Coordinate axes with a blue circle of radius one centered at one comma zero, which is r equals two cosine theta, and an orange circle of radius one centered at zero comma one, which is r equals two sine theta. Both pass through the origin and through one comma one. Their lens-shaped overlap R is shaded tan. A dashed green ray from the origin at angle pi over four passes through one comma one. Two solid green segments from the origin show sample rays: one below the diagonal ends on the orange circle, one above it ends on the blue circle.
            </p>
          </description>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-hw5o-two-circles">
            <diagram dimensions="(320,320)" margins="10">
              <definition>a(t) = (2*cos(t)*cos(t), 2*cos(t)*sin(t))</definition>
              <definition>b(t) = (2*sin(t)*cos(t), 2*sin(t)*sin(t))</definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-0.55,-0.75,2.95,2.75)">
